k about these the  implications these sanctions uh uh are  having the rubles plummeting first of  all uh what does that mean for the  russian economy  well it's it's a disaster in in many  ways if you the pictures we've just seen  of these queues outside banks tell tell  us so much  um if you imagine that you're a russian  saver an ordinary russian who's got all  their savings in rubles they're worth 30  less today than they were yesterday um  it's 119 rubles to the dollar now so in  that case it's it's a very very serious  problem and you know often when we're  talking about you know the political and  the economic where they overlap i think  it's always with sort of things like  currency values that you really see  the political impact the possible  political impact of this because it's so  strong and prices will rise because it  will force inflation because a lot of  the economy relies on imports which will  now cost more because the ruble is worth  less  so in in these areas you can sort of see  that it's going to have massive impact  um the uh sanctions on the the russian  central bank  is that sort of the the sharpest weapon  that has been employed it is i mean  there's been quite a lot of discussion  about the swift transfer payments uh  system being a  a strong weapon but actually in many  ways the the limitation and the  restrictions on the european central  bank are even tougher because what it  means is that um russia has something  like 630 billion in foreign reserves  which um in the run-up to the invasion  was sort of seen as as um as the war  chest that putin would use for this but  around 40 of those are held overseas and  if they can't access 40 of their  reserves that puts them in into a  difficult position so the restrictions  on the central banks have been a very  important weapon another important  weapon  is of course the exclusion from this  swift banking payment and information uh  system that's used globally  in which way does that affect th